The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For any $p > 1$, we use the inequality $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$, which holds because for non-negative terms, $\sum |a_k|^p \leq (\sum |a_k|)^p$. Taking the $1/p$-th power gives $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$. Since $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$, it follows that $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$. Thus, convergence in $\ell_1$ implies convergence in $\ell_p$ for all $p > 1$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

