All solid convex polygons are homeomorphic to each other. A solid convex polygon refers to the filled-in 2D shape of a convex polygon, which is a compact, connected, and contractible subset of the plane. By the Jordan curve theorem, the boundary of any convex polygon is a simple closed curve homeomorphic to a circle. The solid polygon, including its boundary, is therefore homeomorphic to the closed unit disk. Since all closed disks are homeomorphic to each other, all solid convex polygons share this topological structure.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{\text{Yes, all solid convex polygons are homeomorphic to the closed disk.}}

